WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.568
Security values need randomness designed
to resist prediction.

2
00:00:03.568 --> 00:00:05.734
Use the platform's cryptographically
secure facilities

3
00:00:05.734 --> 00:00:08.441
for keys, unpredictable tokens, and random
nonces

4
00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.787
when the construction calls for them.

5
00:00:10.787 --> 00:00:14.486
Node.js provides secure key generation and
random bytes, while Web

6
00:00:14.486 --> 00:00:17.915
Crypto provides appropriate key generation
and secure random APIs.

7
00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:22.337
Node's random UUID function is useful for
suitable identifiers, but a UUID

8
00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:26.849
is not a universal replacement for an
algorithm's key or nonce requirements.

9
00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:29.736
Do not use Math dot random for
cryptographic

10
00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:32.985
keys, reset tokens, or security sensitive
nonce generation.

11
00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:36.053
Human readable phrases, account
identifiers, and timestamps are

12
00:00:36.053 --> 00:00:38.760
also poor substitutes for properly
generated keys.

13
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.189
Follow the algorithm's required key length
and generation mechanism.

14
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:45.528
If a human password must be the source,
use

15
00:00:45.528 --> 00:00:48.776
a purpose built password derivation
process rather than simply

16
00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:52.205
hashing it into a buffer of the desired
size.

17
00:00:52.205 --> 00:00:56.717
A nonce is an input whose required
behavior depends on the construction.

18
00:00:56.717 --> 00:01:01.228
It usually does not need secrecy and can
be stored with ciphertext.

19
00:01:01.228 --> 00:01:04.206
For GCM, reuse of a nonce under the

20
00:01:04.206 --> 00:01:07.274
same key can critically undermine
confidentiality and authentication.

21
00:01:07.274 --> 00:01:10.703
A fixed nonce is therefore not a harmless
simplification.

22
00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:14.403
The uniqueness requirement follows the
actual key material, not merely

23
00:01:14.403 --> 00:01:18.554
a renamed version label or a process that
has just restarted.

24
00:01:18.554 --> 00:01:21.441
Random nonces can be appropriate within
the construction's

25
00:01:21.441 --> 00:01:24.599
limits, but random does not mean
guaranteed unique.

26
00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:28.931
Collision probability, message volume, and
all writers sharing a key matter.

27
00:01:28.931 --> 00:01:31.818
A counter can work only when the
architecture

28
00:01:31.818 --> 00:01:35.518
preserves uniqueness across concurrency,
failures, restarts, and restored backups.

29
00:01:35.518 --> 00:01:38.045
Follow library guidance on nonce size and

30
00:01:38.045 --> 00:01:40.661
per key usage limits, and make allocation

31
00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:43.368
an owned responsibility of the
cryptographic service.

32
00:01:43.368 --> 00:01:46.527
Test operational assumptions, not just the
byte length.

33
00:01:46.527 --> 00:01:49.504
Secure randomness supplies essential
material; correct coordination and

34
00:01:49.504 --> 00:01:52.392
lifecycle rules determine whether that
material is used

35
00:01:52.392 --> 00:01:55.460
safely over the full lifetime of a key.

